course: dictionary
lesson: 11339
title: Zap: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Zap is an informal word, so you will hear it in chats, games and comics more than in school books. Its main meaning is a small electric shock or a quick hit, like a ray gun. Context tells you which meaning is in use.
explain: «Static shocks from a trampoline can make your hair stand up and give a little zap, but they are harmless.» The clues are «little» and «harmless»: a zap here is a small electric shock that does not hurt you.
explain: «When enough charge builds up it can jump across a gap as a spark, like the tiny zap from a door handle.» Here the zap is a tiny spark. Try your meaning in the sentence: a tiny electric spark from a door handle makes sense.
explain: Zap can also mean to heat food in a microwave, or to send something quickly, like zap a message. Check the words around it each time before you decide which meaning fits.
fact: Zap is informal, so it suits chatty writing but may be too casual for a formal essay.
fact: «Zap» can mean to destroy or hit something quickly, as a ray gun does in a space story.
fact: «Zap» can mean to heat food in a microwave, as in zap the leftovers.
fact: «Zap» can be a sound word for a sudden flash or hit: «Zap!»
fact: Static electricity is charge that builds up on you, and it can give a little zap. The zap is usually harmless.
exercise: «Static shocks from a trampoline can give a little zap, but they are harmless.» What is a zap here? | *A small electric shock | A large fire | A loud song | A tasty snack
exercise: Mum says, «Zap the soup for two minutes.» What does zap mean? | Shout loudly | Destroy with a ray gun | Send a message | *Heat in a microwave
exercise: In a space game, the robot zaps the alien ship. What does zap mean? | To tie up slowly | *To destroy quickly | To heat food | To give a gentle hug
test: «When enough charge builds up it can jump across a gap as a spark, like the tiny zap from a door handle.» What does zap mean here? | *A tiny electric spark | A bowl of soup | A long walk | A loud bang like a firework
test: Which word is a sound word for a sudden flash or hit? | Tree | Slowly | Pencil | *Zap
exam: Why is «harmless» a clue to the meaning of zap in «Static shocks from a trampoline can make your hair stand up and give a little zap, but they are harmless.»? | It shows zap is something dangerous that hurts you | *It shows zap is a small shock that does not hurt you | It shows zap is a noun for a flash | It shows zap means heating food
